1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,500
This is DrawingOps: drawing and deliverable control for
architecture and MEP design firms.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:14,879
It works alongside the AutoCAD and Revit tools your teams
already use, and gives everyone one live record of every sheet,

3
00:00:14,879 --> 00:00:17,817
review, transmittal, deadline and fee stage.

4
00:00:18,717 --> 00:00:19,741
The goal is simple.

5
00:00:20,041 --> 00:00:27,209
A principal should be able to see, without phone calls, which
projects are at which stage, which drawings are late or about to

6
00:00:27,209 --> 00:00:30,566
be late, who is overloaded, and what is waiting for review.

7
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:33,518
And the site should never build from a superseded drawing.

8
00:00:34,418 --> 00:00:42,160
This walkthrough uses the interactive DrawingOps prototype,
populated with a fictional firm, Prasad Rao Associates, and

9
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:44,502
fictional projects, people and fees.

10
00:00:44,802 --> 00:00:53,182
Connections to outside systems, such as Microsoft sign-in, email
delivery, the Revit and AutoCAD plugins, and accounting, are

11
00:00:53,182 --> 00:00:58,679
simulated in this demonstration, and each simulation is labelled
where it appears.

12
00:00:59,579 --> 00:01:01,216
Eight roles work in DrawingOps.

13
00:01:01,516 --> 00:01:03,312
The owner watches the portfolio.

14
00:01:03,612 --> 00:01:06,908
Project leads plan projects, fee stages and teams.

15
00:01:07,208 --> 00:01:09,993
Discipline leads plan sheets and do the first check.

16
00:01:10,293 --> 00:01:12,275
Designers move their own sheets forward.

17
00:01:12,575 --> 00:01:14,914
Senior reviewers carry out quality checks.

18
00:01:15,214 --> 00:01:17,064
Document controllers issue drawings.

19
00:01:17,364 --> 00:01:21,653
Accounts receives billing signals, and the administrator
configures the firm.

20
00:01:21,953 --> 00:01:27,743
Clients, consultants and contractors receive drawings through
secure links, with no account needed.

21
00:01:28,643 --> 00:01:30,443
Everything revolves around the sheet.

22
00:01:30,743 --> 00:01:34,450
A sheet exists from the day it is planned, before any file is
drawn.

23
00:01:34,750 --> 00:01:41,736
Every sheet moves through the same states: planned, in progress,
ready for check, in check, approved for issue, and issued.

24
00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:45,702
Issuing a new revision supersedes the old one automatically.

25
00:01:46,002 --> 00:01:50,247
And fee stages move as their sheets move, all the way to ready
to invoice.

26
00:01:51,147 --> 00:01:58,014
We will follow that lifecycle from the owner's dashboard,
through planning, drafting, checking and issuing, out to the

27
00:01:58,014 --> 00:02:03,485
client, and on to billing, the construction stage, reporting,
intelligence and administration.

28
00:02:04,635 --> 00:02:08,702
Chapter one: signing in, and how roles shape what each person
sees.

29
00:02:09,602 --> 00:02:17,607
People sign in with the firm's Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace
account, so there is no new password to remember.

30
00:02:17,907 --> 00:02:25,509
There is deliberately no public sign-up: accounts come from
single sign-on, or from an invitation sent by the administrator.

31
00:02:26,409 --> 00:02:31,025
In this demonstration the Microsoft step is simulated with an
account picker.

32
00:02:31,325 --> 00:02:35,305
In production, people see their firm's own Microsoft sign-in
page.

33
00:02:36,205 --> 00:02:43,445
Administrators, and anyone without a firm account, use an email
and password, protected by a six-digit code from an

34
00:02:43,445 --> 00:02:44,578
authenticator app.

35
00:02:45,478 --> 00:02:50,682
Local accounts can also reset a forgotten password with a code
sent by email.

36
00:02:51,582 --> 00:02:54,799
Once signed in, navigation follows the person's role.

37
00:02:55,099 --> 00:03:01,900
A designer, such as Rahul, sees his own work, projects, reviews,
workload, timesheets, knowledge and contacts.

38
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:08,166
The owner, Nikhil, also sees the portfolio, reports, billing
signals and the audit trail.

39
00:03:08,466 --> 00:03:15,718
Permissions combine an organisation role with a role on each
project, and the same rules apply to every screen, list and

40
00:03:15,718 --> 00:03:16,141
action.

41
00:03:17,291 --> 00:03:19,396
Chapter two: the owner's portfolio.

42
00:03:20,296 --> 00:03:27,687
The portfolio is the owner's home page: every active project in
the firm, with data never more than fifteen minutes old.

43
00:03:28,587 --> 00:03:30,107
Five numbers lead the page.

44
00:03:30,407 --> 00:03:33,085
20 sheets are late, and 22 are at risk.

45
00:03:33,385 --> 00:03:36,032
The oldest review has been waiting 5 days.

46
00:03:36,332 --> 00:03:40,568
2 people over-allocated this week, and 2 stages are ready to
invoice.

47
00:03:40,868 --> 00:03:43,638
Each card also shows the change since last week.

48
00:03:44,538 --> 00:03:47,074
Every number opens the exact list behind it.

49
00:03:47,374 --> 00:03:52,943
Here are the 20 late sheets across the practice, with their
project, owner, state and planned date.

50
00:03:53,243 --> 00:03:55,094
The count always matches the card.

51
00:03:55,994 --> 00:03:57,664
Late and at risk are flags.

52
00:03:57,964 --> 00:04:04,850
They are computed every morning from each sheet's planned issue
date and the firm's working calendar, so nobody has to remember

53
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:05,501
to set them.

54
00:04:06,401 --> 00:04:13,685
Back on the portfolio, the projects table shows each project's
current fee stage, sheets issued against planned, late and

55
00:04:13,685 --> 00:04:17,176
at-risk counts, the project lead, and the next stage date.

56
00:04:17,476 --> 00:04:22,038
Columns sort, and every count opens the project's register,
already filtered.

57
00:04:22,938 --> 00:04:24,968
The forecast column is advisory.

58
00:04:25,268 --> 00:04:28,996
It counts open sheets that a model expects are likely to go
late.

59
00:04:29,296 --> 00:04:31,849
We will return to forecasting later in the walkthrough.

60
00:04:32,749 --> 00:04:40,493
Further down, discipline workload for the next four weeks shows
which teams are over or under capacity, and a forecast lists the

61
00:04:40,493 --> 00:04:45,272
individual sheets most likely to slip, with the reasons behind
each prediction.

62
00:04:46,172 --> 00:04:54,908
Filters narrow the page by project lead or project type, and the
late and at-risk list can be exported to Excel, PDF or CSV.

63
00:04:55,808 --> 00:05:03,126
The bell collects the alerts that matter to this person, such as
the weekly owner summary and stages that are ready to invoice.

64
00:05:04,276 --> 00:05:06,339
Chapter three: the project dashboard.

65
00:05:07,239 --> 00:05:09,221
Opening a project shows its dashboard.

66
00:05:09,521 --> 00:05:15,971
HSR Block B is a hospital for the Navjeevan Health Trust, now in
stage four, working drawings.

67
00:05:16,271 --> 00:05:24,057
Tabs along the top lead to the register, stages, transmittals,
team, activity, client comments, construction and decisions.

68
00:05:24,957 --> 00:05:32,137
The headline numbers show sheets issued against planned, late
and at-risk sheets, sheets waiting for a check, transmittals

69
00:05:32,137 --> 00:05:36,139
that recipients have not yet acknowledged, and open client
comments.

70
00:05:36,439 --> 00:05:37,903
Each one opens its list.

71
00:05:38,803 --> 00:05:45,106
The burn-up chart compares sheets planned with sheets actually
issued, for the whole project or for one discipline.

72
00:05:45,406 --> 00:05:49,934
Every chart can also be shown as a table, for accessibility and
for export.

73
00:05:50,834 --> 00:05:58,023
Below the chart, the stage timeline shows each fee stage with
its share of the fee, its dates and its state, followed by the

74
00:05:58,023 --> 00:05:59,183
list of late sheets.

75
00:06:00,083 --> 00:06:05,517
On the right, the project card holds the client, fee, CAD
versions and the appointment letter.

76
00:06:05,817 --> 00:06:12,348
Each project also has its own email address for client comments,
and a site-view link for the current drawing set.

77
00:06:13,248 --> 00:06:20,755
Further down are this week's team allocation, with anyone over
capacity flagged, the latest transmittals with acknowledgement

78
00:06:20,755 --> 00:06:26,280
counts, open client comments, and an advisory forecast of the
sheets most likely to go late.

79
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:35,765
The project lead or owner can export the whole project at any
time: every record as structured data, plus the issued PDFs, in

80
00:06:35,765 --> 00:06:36,658
one zip file.

81
00:06:36,958 --> 00:06:39,008
The firm's data is never locked in.

82
00:06:40,158 --> 00:06:45,475
Chapter four: projects, fee stages and teams, from the project
lead's point of view.

83
00:06:46,375 --> 00:06:53,940
The projects list shows every project this person can see, with
client, type, current stage, lead, sheets issued against

84
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:55,516
planned, and late counts.

85
00:06:55,816 --> 00:06:59,098
It can be searched, filtered by state and type, and exported.

86
00:06:59,998 --> 00:07:07,856
Creating a project takes a minute: a code in the firm's format,
the name, client, type, location, fee value, dates, the project

87
00:07:07,856 --> 00:07:11,321
lead, the document controller, and a fee-stage template.

88
00:07:12,221 --> 00:07:14,845
Project codes must be unique across the firm.

89
00:07:15,145 --> 00:07:20,902
Reusing the code of HSR Block B is refused, with a message that
says exactly why.

90
00:07:21,802 --> 00:07:29,237
With a fresh code, creating the project lays out its fee stages
from the template, with dates spread across the project period.

91
00:07:30,137 --> 00:07:37,116
The stage plan mirrors the appointment letter: each stage's
share of the fee and its amount, planned dates, the issue

92
00:07:37,116 --> 00:07:41,471
purposes the stage requires, and whether it needs senior quality
control.

93
00:07:42,371 --> 00:07:46,794
Fees must total one hundred percent before the plan can be saved
as final.

94
00:07:47,094 --> 00:07:49,812
Until then it stays a draft, and the total is flagged.

95
00:07:50,712 --> 00:07:56,098
Correcting the figure brings the total back to one hundred
percent, and the plan is saved as final.

96
00:07:56,398 --> 00:07:59,287
Every change to the plan is written to the audit trail.

97
00:08:00,187 --> 00:08:07,830
On a live project such as HSR Block B, the appointment letter is
attached, stages one and two are invoiced with their fee

98
00:08:07,830 --> 00:08:12,693
percentage locked, stage three is ready to invoice, and stage
four is active.

99
00:08:12,993 --> 00:08:16,658
Stages move by themselves as their sheets are started and
issued.

100
00:08:17,558 --> 00:08:24,674
The team tab lists everyone on the project with their project
role and discipline, their hours here this week, and their load

101
00:08:24,674 --> 00:08:25,813
across all projects.

102
00:08:26,113 --> 00:08:31,397
Hours are visible only to the person, their discipline lead, the
project lead and the owner.

103
00:08:32,297 --> 00:08:38,374
Adding someone to the team gives them access to the project, and
they are notified in the app and by email.

104
00:08:39,274 --> 00:08:46,531
The activity feed records every state change, issue,
transmittal, stage event, import and client comment, newest

105
00:08:46,531 --> 00:08:50,613
first, and can be filtered by person, event type or discipline.

106
00:08:51,763 --> 00:08:53,811
Chapter five: the drawing register.

107
00:08:54,711 --> 00:08:57,077
The register is the heart of DrawingOps.

108
00:08:57,377 --> 00:09:05,135
It lists every sheet the firm owes on the project, with number,
title, discipline, package, owner, checker, state, planned date,

109
00:09:05,135 --> 00:09:06,772
current revision and flags.

110
00:09:07,072 --> 00:09:11,636
All one hundred and thirty-two sheets of HSR Block B render
instantly.

111
00:09:12,536 --> 00:09:16,218
Search covers sheet numbers, titles, earlier numbers and owners.

112
00:09:16,518 --> 00:09:20,768
Typing OT duct finds the operating-theatre ductwork sheets
straight away.

113
00:09:21,668 --> 00:09:28,925
Filters combine: discipline, stage, state, owner and package,
plus quick toggles for late or at-risk sheets only.

114
00:09:29,225 --> 00:09:30,938
Here are the late HVAC sheets.

115
00:09:31,838 --> 00:09:38,825
The register can be grouped by discipline, package or stage,
with a running count of issued and late sheets in each group.

116
00:09:39,125 --> 00:09:45,667
Any combination of filters, grouping and sort can be saved as a
named view, and columns can be shown or hidden.

117
00:09:46,567 --> 00:09:50,820
Owners, checkers, packages and planned dates can be edited right
in the table.

118
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:56,122
Each change saves immediately, is audited, and can be undone
from the confirmation.

119
00:09:57,022 --> 00:10:04,797
Selecting several sheets brings up the bulk bar: assign an owner
or a checker, shift planned dates, move sheets into a package,

120
00:10:04,797 --> 00:10:08,225
change their state, issue them, or export the selection.

121
00:10:09,125 --> 00:10:14,375
Shifting dates counts working days on the firm's calendar, so
weekends and holidays are skipped.

122
00:10:14,675 --> 00:10:18,710
Every sheet gets its own audit entry, and the whole change can
be undone.

123
00:10:19,610 --> 00:10:26,670
Changing state in bulk moves only the sheets that are allowed to
move, and reports the others with the reason they were skipped.

124
00:10:27,570 --> 00:10:29,503
A new sheet can be added at any time.

125
00:10:29,803 --> 00:10:36,892
It exists from the day it is planned, before any file, and its
number is checked against the discipline's numbering rule as you

126
00:10:36,892 --> 00:10:37,171
type.

127
00:10:38,071 --> 00:10:43,379
Every list exports to Excel, PDF or CSV with the current filters
applied.

128
00:10:43,679 --> 00:10:49,873
And for people who live in the register, keyboard shortcuts
move, select and open rows without the mouse.

129
00:10:51,023 --> 00:10:54,102
Chapter six: getting sheets and drawings into the system.

130
00:10:55,002 --> 00:10:57,183
Most firms start from an Excel sheet list.

131
00:10:57,483 --> 00:11:05,143
The import wizard accepts an Excel workbook or a CSV file, and
recognises common column headings automatically.

132
00:11:06,043 --> 00:11:08,997
The preview checks every row before anything is created.

133
00:11:09,297 --> 00:11:17,115
This sample contains three deliberate problems: a sheet number
that already exists, an unknown discipline, and a date that does

134
00:11:17,115 --> 00:11:17,731
not exist.

135
00:11:18,031 --> 00:11:19,903
Each is explained in plain words.

136
00:11:20,803 --> 00:11:22,162
Imports are all or nothing.

137
00:11:22,462 --> 00:11:29,595
Once the problem rows are skipped, the valid sheets are created
in one step, and the whole import can be undone for twenty-four

138
00:11:29,595 --> 00:11:32,460
hours, as long as none of the new sheets has moved.

139
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:36,059
Undoing removes the five imported sheets again.

140
00:11:36,959 --> 00:11:38,869
Drawings arrive as PDFs.

141
00:11:39,169 --> 00:11:48,552
Dropping a batch of files matches each one to its sheet by the
number in the file name, or, for scans and badly named files, by

142
00:11:48,552 --> 00:11:57,344
reading the text of the title block: here, a scanned file called
scan_0042 is recognised as F-101 from its title block.

143
00:11:57,644 --> 00:12:01,200
Each file becomes a draft revision with the next revision code.

144
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:09,923
Every file is checked on the way in: it must be a real PDF,
under the size limit, and not a duplicate of a file already

145
00:12:09,923 --> 00:12:10,515
uploaded.

146
00:12:10,815 --> 00:12:17,316
A file whose name breaks the firm's naming rule is kept as a
draft, but blocked from issue until it is renamed.

147
00:12:18,216 --> 00:12:23,034
With the Revit add-in and the AutoCAD plugin, sheet lists sync
straight from the model.

148
00:12:23,334 --> 00:12:26,111
In this demonstration the plugins are simulated.

149
00:12:26,411 --> 00:12:31,029
New sheets arrive as proposals, and nothing is created until a
lead accepts them.

150
00:12:31,329 --> 00:12:35,877
When a title in the model differs from the register, the lead
decides which one wins.

151
00:12:36,777 --> 00:12:39,952
Accepting a proposal creates the sheet in the register.

152
00:12:40,252 --> 00:12:48,767
Alongside, a file-storage connector reports CAD file activity,
so everyone can see when a drawing file was last saved, and by

153
00:12:48,767 --> 00:12:49,108
whom.

154
00:12:50,258 --> 00:12:54,643
Chapter seven: the sheet page, where everything about one
drawing comes together.

155
00:12:55,543 --> 00:12:58,898
Here is M-205, OT ductwork for level two.

156
00:12:59,198 --> 00:13:03,666
The header shows its discipline, stage, package, owner, checker
and planned date.

157
00:13:03,966 --> 00:13:10,903
It is in progress, late by 2 working days, and the advisory
forecast puts its late risk at 97 percent.

158
00:13:11,803 --> 00:13:18,257
The current revision opens in the built-in viewer: a real A3
drawing with the firm's full title block.

159
00:13:18,557 --> 00:13:22,267
It can be downloaded, opened in a new tab, or shown full screen.

160
00:13:23,167 --> 00:13:24,842
Every revision stays on record.

161
00:13:25,142 --> 00:13:32,732
Opening an earlier one shows a clear superseded banner, and
downloading it stamps a diagonal SUPERSEDED watermark and the

162
00:13:32,732 --> 00:13:37,374
date on every page, so an old print is never mistaken for the
current one.

163
00:13:38,274 --> 00:13:45,352
On the right, the reviews card lists every check and senior QC
decision for each revision, with the reviewer and the date.

164
00:13:46,252 --> 00:13:53,564
Who has which revision shows every person and organisation that
received this sheet, which revision they hold, and on which

165
00:13:53,564 --> 00:13:57,844
transmittal, so a superseded copy in someone's hands is never a
mystery.

166
00:13:58,744 --> 00:14:06,865
Below that, the activity timeline shows every state change with
who made it, when, and why, followed by CAD file activity and

167
00:14:06,865 --> 00:14:10,633
the results of AI drawing checks, which we will see later.

168
00:14:11,533 --> 00:14:19,013
The more menu holds the less frequent actions: editing sheet
details, working notes, and, for the project lead, overriding a

169
00:14:19,013 --> 00:14:21,184
workflow step with a written reason.

170
00:14:21,484 --> 00:14:25,449
Even an override stays within the allowed transitions, and is
audited.

171
00:14:26,599 --> 00:14:28,536
Chapter eight: a designer's day.

172
00:14:29,436 --> 00:14:31,198
Rahul is an HVAC designer.

173
00:14:31,498 --> 00:14:39,027
My work lists his sheets across every project by due date, each
with its state, its flags, and the one next action that makes

174
00:14:39,027 --> 00:14:39,991
sense right now.

175
00:14:40,891 --> 00:14:44,691
Starting a planned sheet is a single click at the moment the
work begins.

176
00:14:44,990 --> 00:14:49,490
If it is the first sheet of its stage, the stage becomes active
automatically.

177
00:14:50,390 --> 00:14:57,090
Further down, Returned to you lists sheets that came back from a
check, with who returned them, when, and the checker's comment.

178
00:14:57,390 --> 00:15:03,046
On the right, hours this week compares time logged with
capacity, and flags a missing timesheet.

179
00:15:03,946 --> 00:15:07,882
When a drawing is ready, Rahul opens the sheet and submits it
for check.

180
00:15:08,182 --> 00:15:11,599
Here, M-206 already has a draft check print attached.

181
00:15:12,499 --> 00:15:15,120
Submitting opens the discipline's pre-check list.

182
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:20,003
Mandatory items must be ticked, and the submission is refused
until they are.

183
00:15:20,903 --> 00:15:28,312
With every item ticked, the sheet moves to ready for check, the
ticks are stored with the review, and Karthik, the HVAC checker,

184
00:15:28,312 --> 00:15:29,817
is notified straight away.

185
00:15:30,717 --> 00:15:35,687
A sheet that comes back carries the checker's comment and any
redline files on its page.

186
00:15:35,987 --> 00:15:43,524
M-204 was returned because the return-air path is not allowed
through the corridor ceiling in the ICU zone.

187
00:15:43,824 --> 00:15:46,082
Starting the revision puts it back in progress.

188
00:15:47,232 --> 00:15:49,999
Chapter nine: checking, returning and approving.

189
00:15:50,899 --> 00:15:52,770
Anil leads the architecture team.

190
00:15:53,070 --> 00:15:57,604
His My work shows what is waiting for his check, and how long
the oldest has waited.

191
00:15:58,504 --> 00:16:03,649
The review queue lists every sheet waiting for him, oldest
first, with the working days waited.

192
00:16:03,949 --> 00:16:09,830
Anything open for more than three working days is flagged as
overdue, and the daily sweep escalates it.

193
00:16:10,130 --> 00:16:13,948
A senior QC tag shows that a sheet's stage needs a second check.

194
00:16:14,848 --> 00:16:21,864
Opening a review puts the drawing and the check side by side,
with the designer, the submission time and the review round.

195
00:16:22,764 --> 00:16:28,581
Returning a sheet needs a comment or a redline file, so the
designer always knows what to fix.

196
00:16:28,881 --> 00:16:34,085
Anil writes his comment and returns A-401 to Meghna, who is
notified at once.

197
00:16:34,985 --> 00:16:37,197
For A-107, the check passes.

198
00:16:37,496 --> 00:16:44,710
Because stage four requires senior quality control, Anil's
approval sends the sheet on to the senior reviewer as a second

199
00:16:44,710 --> 00:16:45,008
step.

200
00:16:45,308 --> 00:16:47,261
It stays in check until she approves.

201
00:16:48,161 --> 00:16:49,125
Going on leave?

202
00:16:49,425 --> 00:16:52,887
A reviewer can delegate their queue to a colleague for a date
range.

203
00:16:53,187 --> 00:16:57,843
Reviews route to the delegate automatically, and overdue
escalations follow them.

204
00:16:58,743 --> 00:17:06,554
The discipline dashboard gives Anil one view of his team: late
and at-risk sheets, the check queue, sheets approved but not yet

205
00:17:06,554 --> 00:17:10,490
issued, assignments per person, and capacity this week and next.

206
00:17:11,390 --> 00:17:16,936
Sunita, the senior reviewer, now finds A-107 in her queue,
tagged senior QC.

207
00:17:17,836 --> 00:17:20,463
Her approval moves the sheet to approved for issue.

208
00:17:20,763 --> 00:17:25,229
The sheet's owner and the document controller are both notified
that it is ready.

209
00:17:26,379 --> 00:17:29,125
Chapter ten: issuing drawings with transmittals.

210
00:17:30,025 --> 00:17:31,978
Deepa is the document controller.

211
00:17:32,278 --> 00:17:36,901
Her My work lists the approved sheets that are ready to issue,
project by project.

212
00:17:37,801 --> 00:17:42,510
The issue composer has three steps: sheets, purpose and
recipients, and a preview.

213
00:17:42,810 --> 00:17:48,285
The next transmittal number is reserved in sequence for the
project, and numbers are never reused.

214
00:17:49,185 --> 00:17:52,904
Each approved sheet shows its draft PDF and whether it is ready.

215
00:17:53,204 --> 00:18:00,003
A-104 is blocked because its file name breaks the naming rule,
and E-102 has no PDF yet.

216
00:18:00,903 --> 00:18:05,614
A-104 is fixed with one click, which renames the file to the
valid name.

217
00:18:05,914 --> 00:18:12,808
Choosing all approved sheets then selects everything that is
ready, and E-102 waits behind for its PDF.

218
00:18:13,708 --> 00:18:15,701
The purpose of the issue is required.

219
00:18:16,001 --> 00:18:17,582
Here it is good for construction.

220
00:18:17,882 --> 00:18:25,603
Recipients receive a secure link by email, paper copies handed
over in person can be recorded too, and a note can be added for

221
00:18:25,603 --> 00:18:26,154
everyone.

222
00:18:27,054 --> 00:18:34,644
The preview shows the numbered cover sheet exactly as recipients
will receive it: the project, purpose, recipients, and every

223
00:18:34,644 --> 00:18:36,223
drawing with its revision.

224
00:18:37,123 --> 00:18:39,487
Issuing asks for one final confirmation.

225
00:18:40,387 --> 00:18:43,283
One click issues the whole set on a single transmittal.

226
00:18:43,583 --> 00:18:50,613
In the same step, earlier revisions of those sheets are
superseded, recipients get their secure link, and anyone who

227
00:18:50,613 --> 00:18:53,885
received an older revision is told a newer one exists.

228
00:18:54,785 --> 00:19:02,223
The transmittal log lists every issue with its purpose, sheets,
recipients and acknowledgement status, including emails that

229
00:19:02,223 --> 00:19:05,042
bounced, so nothing is assumed to have arrived.

230
00:19:05,942 --> 00:19:11,241
Switching to the matrix view shows, for every sheet, which
revision each recipient holds.

231
00:19:12,141 --> 00:19:19,845
Each transmittal has its own page, with delivery, download and
acknowledgement times for every recipient, and a resend button

232
00:19:19,845 --> 00:19:21,078
for a bounced email.

233
00:19:21,378 --> 00:19:27,973
Transmittals are never edited after sending: a correction is a
new transmittal that references the original.

234
00:19:29,123 --> 00:19:32,970
Chapter eleven: what the client, consultant or contractor sees.

235
00:19:33,270 --> 00:19:35,536
No account, and no software to install.

236
00:19:36,436 --> 00:19:39,689
Recipients get an ordinary email in their own inbox.

237
00:19:39,989 --> 00:19:45,223
In this demonstration, emails land in a demonstration mailbox
instead of being sent.

238
00:19:46,123 --> 00:19:53,897
The link opens a secure page with no sign-in: the transmittal
number, project, purpose, sender and expiry date, and every

239
00:19:53,897 --> 00:19:55,568
drawing with its revision.

240
00:19:56,468 --> 00:20:02,126
Drawings download one at a time, or all together as a zip file
that includes the cover sheet.

241
00:20:03,026 --> 00:20:08,648
Acknowledging receipt records the date and time, and the sender
sees it on the transmittal immediately.

242
00:20:09,548 --> 00:20:16,840
People who hold an older revision are emailed automatically when
a newer one is issued, and told to stop using the old one.

243
00:20:17,740 --> 00:20:24,897
On site, the project's site-view link shows only the current
issued set, on a phone, with search and large touch targets.

244
00:20:25,197 --> 00:20:29,142
Superseded drawings never appear, so nobody builds from an old
sheet.

245
00:20:30,042 --> 00:20:34,379
Back in DrawingOps, the client's acknowledgement already shows
on the transmittal.

246
00:20:35,529 --> 00:20:40,058
Chapter twelve: client comments, decisions, and client approval
of a stage.

247
00:20:40,958 --> 00:20:45,695
Client comments arrive by email, letter, phone call, WhatsApp or
in meetings.

248
00:20:45,995 --> 00:20:52,310
Each one is logged with a number, linked to the sheets it
affects, assigned to a person, and tracked until it has a

249
00:20:52,310 --> 00:20:53,244
written response.

250
00:20:54,144 --> 00:21:01,515
A comment with no response after five days is flagged as
overdue, and the daily sweep reminds the person it is assigned

251
00:21:01,515 --> 00:21:03,063
to, and the project lead.

252
00:21:03,963 --> 00:21:10,707
Logging a new comment takes seconds: what the client asked for,
where it came from, the sheets it concerns, and who should

253
00:21:10,707 --> 00:21:11,260
answer it.

254
00:21:11,560 --> 00:21:13,471
The person is notified straight away.

255
00:21:14,371 --> 00:21:17,172
A comment can only be closed with a written response.

256
00:21:17,472 --> 00:21:21,239
The answer becomes part of the project record, and is searchable
later.

257
00:21:23,438 --> 00:21:26,220
Each project also has its own email address.

258
00:21:26,520 --> 00:21:31,256
Forwarding a client's email to it logs the comment
automatically, matched to the sender.

259
00:21:31,556 --> 00:21:34,332
In this demonstration the forwarding is simulated.

260
00:21:35,232 --> 00:21:42,539
Emails from senders the system does not recognise wait in a
review queue, so nothing is logged against a project until a

261
00:21:42,539 --> 00:21:43,635
person accepts it.

262
00:21:44,535 --> 00:21:49,982
Decisions agreed with the client are recorded too, with their
context and the sheets they affect.

263
00:21:50,282 --> 00:21:55,439
They are searchable across projects, so the reasoning behind a
design choice is never lost.

264
00:21:56,865 --> 00:22:02,402
On the Mysuru heritage hotel, every deliverable of stage five,
tender, has been issued.

265
00:22:02,702 --> 00:22:08,170
The project lead now records the client's approval, attaching
the approval letter as evidence.

266
00:22:09,070 --> 00:22:16,309
With every deliverable issued and the client's approval on
record, the stage becomes ready to invoice automatically, and

267
00:22:16,309 --> 00:22:19,566
Accounts, the owner and the project lead are notified.

268
00:22:20,716 --> 00:22:23,480
Chapter thirteen: billing signals, for Accounts.

269
00:22:24,380 --> 00:22:31,559
First, the administrator connects the firm's accounting system:
choose the columns to send, give a webhook address, and switch

270
00:22:31,559 --> 00:22:31,901
it on.

271
00:22:32,201 --> 00:22:36,030
The address is checked, so it can never point inside the firm's
own network.

272
00:22:36,330 --> 00:22:39,232
In this demonstration the connection is simulated.

273
00:22:40,132 --> 00:22:44,780
Meera, in Accounts, sees the stages that are ready to invoice on
her home page.

274
00:22:45,680 --> 00:22:52,858
Billing signals lists every stage ready to invoice, with the
project, the stage, its share of the fee and the amount, how

275
00:22:52,858 --> 00:22:56,418
long it has been waiting, and the client approval behind it.

276
00:22:56,718 --> 00:23:00,019
The Mysuru stage approved a moment ago is already here.

277
00:23:00,919 --> 00:23:08,549
The list exports as a CSV file in the columns the accounting
tool expects, or pushed straight to the accounting system as a

278
00:23:08,549 --> 00:23:09,480
signed message.

279
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:14,381
In this demonstration the push is simulated, and the message is
shown instead of sent.

280
00:23:15,281 --> 00:23:22,257
Once the invoice is raised in the accounting tool, Accounts
marks the stage invoiced with its reference and date, and the

281
00:23:22,257 --> 00:23:23,929
stage's fee percentage locks.

282
00:23:24,829 --> 00:23:27,710
Invoiced stages stay on record with their reference.

283
00:23:28,010 --> 00:23:35,435
Invoicing, tax and ledgers deliberately remain in the firm's
accounting software: DrawingOps supplies the signal, not the

284
00:23:35,435 --> 00:23:35,926
invoice.

285
00:23:36,826 --> 00:23:45,486
Accounts sees projects in a deliberately narrow way: stages and
fees only, never drawings, reviews or individual people's hours.

286
00:23:46,636 --> 00:23:49,086
Chapter fourteen: workload and timesheets.

287
00:23:49,986 --> 00:23:56,342
Workload is a heat map of planned hours against capacity, person
by person, for the next eight weeks.

288
00:23:56,642 --> 00:24:04,715
Above one hundred and ten percent is flagged as over-allocated,
and below fifty percent as under-allocated, always with an icon

289
00:24:04,715 --> 00:24:06,558
and a word as well as colour.

290
00:24:07,458 --> 00:24:13,096
Filters narrow the map to a discipline, a project, or only the
people who are over or under.

291
00:24:13,396 --> 00:24:14,822
Here is the HVAC team.

292
00:24:15,722 --> 00:24:21,268
Clicking a cell shows where that person's hours go, project by
project and stage by stage.

293
00:24:21,568 --> 00:24:25,582
Hours can be edited in place, or copied forward to the next
three weeks.

294
00:24:26,482 --> 00:24:33,140
New allocations are added for a person, a project stage and a
number of weeks, and the heat map updates at once.

295
00:24:34,040 --> 00:24:38,669
The staff directory holds each person's discipline, weekly
capacity and leave.

296
00:24:38,969 --> 00:24:44,464
Leave and the firm's holidays reduce capacity automatically, so
the heat map stays honest.

297
00:24:45,364 --> 00:24:50,710
Timesheets are weekly, by project and stage, and start from the
person's own allocations.

298
00:24:51,010 --> 00:24:52,888
Rahul enters his hours for the week.

299
00:24:53,188 --> 00:24:57,253
People who track time elsewhere can import a CSV file instead.

300
00:24:58,153 --> 00:25:00,849
Submitting the week locks it and sends it for approval.

301
00:25:01,149 --> 00:25:04,512
A timesheet missing by Tuesday noon raises a reminder.

302
00:25:05,412 --> 00:25:12,796
Discipline leads and project leads approve submitted timesheets,
or return them with a reason, and can remind anyone whose week

303
00:25:12,796 --> 00:25:13,784
is still missing.

304
00:25:14,084 --> 00:25:20,448
Individual hours are only ever visible to the person, their
discipline lead, the project lead and the owner.

305
00:25:21,598 --> 00:25:26,119
Chapter fifteen: notifications, alerts, and the automations
behind them.

306
00:25:27,019 --> 00:25:29,316
Everyone has one notification centre.

307
00:25:29,616 --> 00:25:35,201
Rahul's shows his assignments, review results and deadline
alerts, each linking straight to the sheet.

308
00:25:36,101 --> 00:25:42,246
Preferences decide how each kind of alert arrives: instantly, in
a daily digest, or not at all.

309
00:25:42,546 --> 00:25:47,266
Alerts that must not be missed, such as a sheet returned with
redlines, are locked on.

310
00:25:47,566 --> 00:25:50,126
Quiet hours hold notifications overnight.

311
00:25:51,026 --> 00:25:55,170
Everything set to digest arrives together in one email each
morning.

312
00:25:56,070 --> 00:26:04,257
Behind the alerts are ten automation rules, all visible to the
administrator in one place: at-risk and late flags, superseding

313
00:26:04,257 --> 00:26:11,729
on issue, naming checks, allocation and timesheet checks,
overdue reviews and client comments, the ready-to-invoice

314
00:26:11,729 --> 00:26:13,418
signal, and RFI due dates.

315
00:26:13,718 --> 00:26:15,746
Each has its schedule and its threshold.

316
00:26:16,646 --> 00:26:18,843
The daily sweep runs at eight every morning.

317
00:26:19,143 --> 00:26:23,151
Running it now sends the reminders and escalations that are due
today.

318
00:26:24,051 --> 00:26:26,538
Running it a second time sends nothing twice.

319
00:26:26,838 --> 00:26:30,879
Every alert goes out once per day, and the duplicates are
counted and skipped.

320
00:26:31,179 --> 00:26:32,736
People can trust their inbox.

321
00:26:33,636 --> 00:26:40,852
The run log records every sweep with its counts and duration,
and a warning appears if the morning sweep ever fails to run.

322
00:26:41,752 --> 00:26:49,564
And every Monday morning the owner receives a weekly summary by
email: late and at-risk sheets, overloaded people, and money

323
00:26:49,564 --> 00:26:51,013
waiting to be invoiced.

324
00:26:52,163 --> 00:26:54,549
Chapter sixteen: the construction stage.

325
00:26:55,449 --> 00:27:00,726
Once a project is on site, the construction tab brings the
site's paperwork into the same system.

326
00:27:01,026 --> 00:27:04,179
Koramangala Tech Park is in stage six, construction.

327
00:27:04,479 --> 00:27:12,429
RFIs are logged with the question, who raised it, whose court
the ball is in, the due date, the linked sheets and any cost or

328
00:27:12,429 --> 00:27:13,192
time impact.

329
00:27:13,492 --> 00:27:16,251
RFIs due within two working days are flagged.

330
00:27:17,151 --> 00:27:24,566
Answering an RFI can start the next revision of every linked
sheet in the same step, so a drawing change is never forgotten.

331
00:27:26,403 --> 00:27:34,166
Submittals from contractors, such as shop drawings, samples and
product data, are reviewed by the right person and stamped:

332
00:27:34,166 --> 00:27:38,079
approved, approved as noted, revise and resubmit, or rejected.

333
00:27:38,379 --> 00:27:40,547
The stamped PDF is ready to send back.

334
00:27:41,447 --> 00:27:48,128
Site visit reports are filed from a phone: who attended, the
weather, observations linked to sheets, and photos.

335
00:27:48,428 --> 00:27:54,778
Photos are compressed and their location data is removed, and
every report exports as a PDF.

336
00:27:55,678 --> 00:28:01,851
Change orders record cost and time, where they came from, often
an RFI, and the sheets they affect.

337
00:28:02,151 --> 00:28:07,220
The project lead approves or rejects them, and the approved
total is always up to date.

338
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:16,297
Clash results from Navisworks, or any tool that exports BCF, are
imported as issues with their viewpoints, disciplines, sheets,

339
00:28:16,297 --> 00:28:17,585
assignee and status.

340
00:28:17,885 --> 00:28:23,575
Importing again updates the existing issues by their unique ID,
instead of duplicating them.

341
00:28:24,725 --> 00:28:27,285
Chapter seventeen: finding anything, fast.

342
00:28:28,185 --> 00:28:31,092
Command search opens from anywhere with Control K.

343
00:28:31,392 --> 00:28:39,355
Typing a sheet number finds it on every project the person can
see, and the same box finds projects, transmittals and contacts,

344
00:28:39,355 --> 00:28:40,734
and jumps to any page.

345
00:28:41,634 --> 00:28:49,164
Words work too: OT duct finds the operating-theatre ductwork
sheets on both hospital projects, and the search reports how

346
00:28:49,164 --> 00:28:49,973
long it took.

347
00:28:50,273 --> 00:28:53,319
Results only ever include projects the person can see.

348
00:28:54,219 --> 00:28:55,727
Knowledge search goes deeper.

349
00:28:56,027 --> 00:29:03,986
It searches decisions, client comments, RFI answers and review
comments, highlights the matching words, and links every result

350
00:29:03,986 --> 00:29:05,628
to its project and sheets.

351
00:29:05,928 --> 00:29:13,427
Here, OT ceiling HEPA finds the review comment, the client
comment and the decision behind the operating-theatre ceilings.

352
00:29:14,327 --> 00:29:21,521
Contacts hold every outside person and organisation, by kind:
client, consultant, contractor or authority.

353
00:29:21,821 --> 00:29:26,479
Adding someone whose email already exists raises a warning, so
the list stays clean.

354
00:29:27,629 --> 00:29:29,328
Chapter eighteen: reports.

355
00:29:30,228 --> 00:29:37,557
The baseline report tracks the four measures the firm agreed to
improve: the share of sheets issued after their planned date,

356
00:29:37,557 --> 00:29:44,945
the average working days a sheet spends in check, hours logged
against hours planned, and the days from deliverables issued to

357
00:29:44,945 --> 00:29:45,414
invoice.

358
00:29:46,314 --> 00:29:53,806
Each measure is broken down by month and by project, for any
date range, with the definitions printed on the report itself.

359
00:29:54,106 --> 00:29:57,404
The report downloads as a PDF for the monthly review.

360
00:29:58,304 --> 00:30:03,544
Hours by project and stage compares planned and logged hours for
every fee stage.

361
00:30:04,444 --> 00:30:10,005
Utilisation compares billable hours with available hours, by
discipline and by person.

362
00:30:10,305 --> 00:30:15,955
People are listed alphabetically, never ranked: DrawingOps is
not a productivity league table.

363
00:30:16,855 --> 00:30:23,509
Review cycle shows the average working days in check by
discipline and by checker, and how long sheets spend in each

364
00:30:23,509 --> 00:30:23,853
state.

365
00:30:24,153 --> 00:30:27,461
And the missing timesheets list sends reminders with one click.

366
00:30:28,611 --> 00:30:30,479
Chapter nineteen: intelligence.

367
00:30:30,779 --> 00:30:38,269
Every forecast and AI check in DrawingOps is advisory, clearly
labelled, and switched on only after the firm has built up

368
00:30:38,269 --> 00:30:39,879
enough of its own history.

369
00:30:40,779 --> 00:30:47,897
A model scores every open sheet's risk of going late, and
explains its reasons: working days left, progress so far, the

370
00:30:47,897 --> 00:30:54,238
owner's load this week, the number of review rounds, and how
often that discipline's sheets have run late.

371
00:30:54,538 --> 00:30:56,505
Forecasts never change any data.

372
00:30:57,405 --> 00:31:04,596
The workload forecast projects each discipline's hours for the
next eight weeks beside planned allocations and capacity, and

373
00:31:04,596 --> 00:31:08,366
shows its own measured error, so people know how far to trust
it.

374
00:31:09,266 --> 00:31:17,422
AI drawing checks read the title blocks and cross-references of
uploaded PDFs, and raise findings, such as an empty checked-by

375
00:31:17,422 --> 00:31:21,889
field, or a callout that points to a sheet missing from the
register.

376
00:31:22,189 --> 00:31:23,908
Each finding shows its confidence.

377
00:31:24,808 --> 00:31:27,484
Nothing changes until a person decides.

378
00:31:27,784 --> 00:31:33,935
Accepting a finding records the decision and tells the sheet's
owner; dismissing it is recorded too.

379
00:31:34,835 --> 00:31:39,191
The administrator controls each capability, off, evaluation or
on.

380
00:31:39,491 --> 00:31:46,956
They unlock only after a gate: at least six months of clean
history and two thousand issued revisions, with back-tested

381
00:31:46,956 --> 00:31:49,214
accuracy shown against simple rules.

382
00:31:49,514 --> 00:31:55,019
In this demonstration, the history, accuracy and forecast
figures are simulated stand-ins.

383
00:31:56,169 --> 00:31:58,168
Chapter twenty: administration.

384
00:31:59,068 --> 00:32:06,815
Administration starts with the organisation: the firm's name and
address, time zone, project code format, default weekly

385
00:32:06,815 --> 00:32:14,045
capacity, digest time, how long transmittal links stay valid,
and when an unacknowledged transmittal is flagged.

386
00:32:14,345 --> 00:32:18,014
Dates, times and rupee amounts follow Indian formats throughout.

387
00:32:18,914 --> 00:32:23,172
Disciplines each carry their own sheet-number rule, with a live
test beside it.

388
00:32:24,072 --> 00:32:30,440
Stage templates mirror the firm's standard appointment letters,
with their fee percentages, and one is the default.

389
00:32:31,340 --> 00:32:36,189
Revision codes follow the firm's scheme, P for preliminary and C
for construction.

390
00:32:36,489 --> 00:32:44,449
A purpose is required on every issue, and ISO 19650 suitability
codes are available for firms that use them.

391
00:32:45,349 --> 00:32:47,368
Naming rules come with a live tester.

392
00:32:47,668 --> 00:32:54,243
Paste a file name, and see immediately whether it passes, and if
not, exactly why, with a valid example.

393
00:32:55,143 --> 00:33:02,553
Pre-check checklists are defined for each discipline and stage,
with their mandatory items, and the working calendar sets the

394
00:33:02,553 --> 00:33:07,888
working week and holidays that every deadline, escalation and
working-day count relies on.

395
00:33:08,788 --> 00:33:14,764
Users and roles shows each person's organisation role, how they
sign in, and when they were last seen.

396
00:33:15,064 --> 00:33:19,604
People can be deactivated, but never deleted, so their history
stays intact.

397
00:33:19,904 --> 00:33:21,883
New people are invited by email.

398
00:33:22,783 --> 00:33:24,774
The invitation arrives by email.

399
00:33:25,074 --> 00:33:32,769
The new person chooses a password, or signs in with the firm's
Microsoft account, and sees projects once a project lead adds

400
00:33:32,769 --> 00:33:33,700
them to a team.

401
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:42,713
Integrations lists every connection with its status: single
sign-on, local accounts with two-factor, outbound and inbound

402
00:33:42,713 --> 00:33:49,082
email, the Revit and AutoCAD plugins, the file-storage
connector, clash import, and accounting.

403
00:33:49,382 --> 00:33:53,253
Scoped API tokens are created here and shown only once.

404
00:33:53,553 --> 00:33:59,524
Options that depend on the firm's tools, such as Autodesk Forma
and Bluebeam, are marked as conditional.

405
00:34:00,424 --> 00:34:08,675
Onboarding loads a live project from a template workbook and a
folder of issued PDFs, including historic revisions, without

406
00:34:08,675 --> 00:34:10,687
sending a single notification.

407
00:34:10,987 --> 00:34:14,453
Problems in the workbook are listed before anything is loaded.

408
00:34:15,353 --> 00:34:22,619
Finally, the audit trail records every change: who made it,
when, what it was before and after, from which address, with a

409
00:34:22,619 --> 00:34:23,274
request ID.

410
00:34:23,574 --> 00:34:30,799
Rows can never be edited or deleted, and the full trail exports
as a background job that emails a link when it is ready.

411
00:34:31,949 --> 00:34:35,393
Chapter twenty-one: the qualities that run through the whole
product.

412
00:34:36,293 --> 00:34:43,159
Every screen also works in a dark theme, chosen from the account
menu, and in a compact or comfortable row height.

413
00:34:44,059 --> 00:34:48,723
Status is always shown with colour, an icon and a word, never
colour alone.

414
00:34:49,023 --> 00:34:53,749
Every chart has a table view, and keyboard shortcuts cover the
most frequent actions.

415
00:34:54,649 --> 00:34:58,708
Underneath, DrawingOps is designed so that the record can be
trusted.

416
00:34:59,008 --> 00:35:05,727
Permissions are enforced on the server for every request, for
each person's organisation role and project role.

417
00:35:06,027 --> 00:35:08,697
Issued files are never altered or overwritten.

418
00:35:08,997 --> 00:35:10,739
The audit trail is insert-only.

419
00:35:11,039 --> 00:35:13,051
Automations are safe to run twice.

420
00:35:13,351 --> 00:35:16,405
And a project's full record can be exported at any time.

421
00:35:17,305 --> 00:35:24,783
DrawingOps gives an architecture and MEP firm one live record of
every sheet, from the day it is planned to the day it is

422
00:35:24,783 --> 00:35:30,902
invoiced: who owns it, where it stands, what is late, who is
overloaded, and what the client holds.

423
00:35:31,202 --> 00:35:36,896
It works alongside the CAD tools the firm already uses, and it
keeps the site on the current set.

424
00:35:37,796 --> 00:35:39,975
DrawingOps is built by LatentIQ.

425
00:35:40,275 --> 00:35:45,113
To see it working with your own projects and sheet lists, talk
to the LatentIQ team.

426
00:35:45,413 --> 00:35:46,627
Thank you for watching.

